{"name":"Gold Perennial","hex":"#caaf81","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"},{"term":"perennial","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Lasting or remaining active throughout the year, for multiple years, or all the time.","Continuing without cessation or intermission for several years, or for an undetermined or infinite period; never-ending or never failing; perpetual, unceasing."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant that is active throughout the year, or has a life cycle of more than two growing seasons.","A thing that lasts forever.\nA person or thing (such as a problem) that appears or returns regularly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/perennial"}],"entries":[{"title":"Perennial","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, the term perennial is used to differentiate a plant from shorter-lived annuals and biennials. It has thus been defined as a plant that lives more than 2 years. The term is also loosely used to distinguish plants with little or no woody growth from trees and shrubs, which are also technically perennials. An estimated 94% of plant species are perennials."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Cykoria_podroznik_pokroj.jpg/330px-Cykoria_podroznik_pokroj.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perennial","wikidata":"Q157957"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/RIMG0163uf.JPG/500px-RIMG0163uf.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/RIMG0163uf.JPG/1280px-RIMG0163uf.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/RIMG0163uf.JPG/500px-RIMG0163uf.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dahlia plants are tender perennials that originate from climates that are warm all year round and need special care to survive cold winters.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARIMG0163uf.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Switchgrass_roots.jpg/500px-Switchgrass_roots.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Switchgrass_roots.jpg/1280px-Switchgrass_roots.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Switchgrass_roots.jpg/500px